{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdZ3z1kfZp5Vj6EXwbgF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Zest began testing its text-message restaurant concierge last month and held the public launch until it could add reservations. The company started as an app that builds personalized maps and lists from users' food and drink card transactions. On Thursday it released the concierge with booking through OpenTable, Resy and SevenRooms.\n\nThe delay suggests the company considered booking necessary to the product. A recommendation in a text thread would still leave the user to find the restaurant elsewhere and check for a table. With booking built in, the conversation can run from choosing a place to a confirmed reservation. That includes group chats, where Zest blends members' tastes, runs a vote on the options, and books for everyone without leaving the thread. TechCrunch's tests found the suggestions fairly well matched to its tastes and to conditions like time of day, party size and location. TechCrunch also noted that finding an open table is something other apps have offered for years, so the gain is the convenience of settling it in one conversation.\n\nRevenue follows the same path. CEO Mario Gomez-Hall calls booking and ordering the first revenue lines, with food ordering still to come, and says \"the agent is the product now.\" The article reports that Zest has thousands of users and gives no revenue figures, so how much these transactions will earn is not yet known."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1396,"cache_read_input_tokens":2766,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1396},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":nu